---
title: Simulations
description: Programmatically trigger drag animations with DndSimulator for undo/redo, onboarding, or demo sequences.
---

# Simulations

The `DndSimulator` lets you trigger drag animations programmatically — without any user interaction and without firing drop events. Useful for undo/redo flows, onboarding tutorials, or demo sequences.

## Access

`DndSimulator` is available in two ways:

**Via `DndController` (recommended):**

```ts
await controller.simulateReturn(...)
await controller.simulateDrop(...)
```

**Directly (advanced):**

```ts
import { DndSimulator } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';
```

The controller's methods are thin delegates to `DndSimulator` internally.

---

## simulateReturn

```ts
controller.simulateReturn(
    itemId: string,
    fromContainerId: string,
    toContainerId: string,
    toPosition: number
): Promise<void>
```

Animates an item flying from its current DOM position to a destination. Returns a promise that resolves when the animation completes.

- When `fromContainerId === toContainerId` — uses a scroll-aware return animation (same as a cancelled drag)
- When containers differ — uses a fly-to-target animation

**Does not fire any drop events.**

### Example — undo a move

```ts
// User moved item '3' from 'list-a' to 'list-b'. Undo it:
await controller.simulateReturn('3', 'list-b', 'list-a', originalIndex);

// After the animation completes, update your data model:
moveItem('3', 'list-b', 'list-a', originalIndex);
```

---

## simulateDrop

```ts
controller.simulateDrop(
    itemId: string,
    fromContainerId: string,
    toContainerId: string,
    toPosition: number,
    options?: SimulateOptions
): Promise<void>

interface SimulateOptions {
    /** When true, fires onDrop after the animation completes. Default: false */
    emitEvents?: boolean;
}
```

Animates an item flying from its current DOM position to a target container and position. Always uses a fly-to-target animation regardless of whether containers match.

By default **does not fire any drop events**. Pass `{ emitEvents: true }` to fire `onDrop` after the animation completes.

### Example — auto-sort animation

```ts
// Animate item '2' flying to position 0 in the same list:
await controller.simulateDrop('2', 'list', 'list', 0);

// Then update your data:
moveItemToTop('2');
```

### Example — programmatic move with events

```ts
// Animate AND fire onDrop (your existing handler updates the data model):
await controller.simulateDrop('task-3', 'backlog', 'in-progress', 0, { emitEvents: true });
// onDrop fires automatically — no manual data update needed
```

---

## simulateSwap

```ts
controller.simulateSwap(
    idA: string,
    containerA: string,
    idB: string,
    containerB: string,
    duration?: number  // default: 300ms
): Promise<void>
```

Animates two items swapping positions simultaneously using direct CSS transforms. Both elements animate at the same time without going through the ghost system.

**The caller is responsible for updating data state after the promise resolves.**

### Example — swap two items

```ts
await controller.simulateSwap('item-1', 'list', 'item-5', 'list');

// After animation — update your data:
const i = items.findIndex(x => x.id === 'item-1');
const j = items.findIndex(x => x.id === 'item-5');
[items[i], items[j]] = [items[j], items[i]];
items = [...items];
```

---

## simulateBatchSwap

```ts
controller.simulateBatchSwap(
    ids: string[],
    applyState: () => void | Promise<void>,
    duration?: number  // default: 300ms
): Promise<void>
```

Animates multiple items simultaneously to their new positions using FLIP (First, Last, Invert, Play). Works across different containers. All elements animate at the same time.

Pass an `applyState` callback that updates your data model — the library records DOM positions before and after the state change, then animates the delta.

### Example — sort all items at once

```ts
const sorted = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label));

await controller.simulateBatchSwap(
    sorted.map(i => i.id),
    () => { items = sorted }
);
```

### Example — swap all items between two containers

```ts
const oldA = [...teamA];
const oldB = [...teamB];

await controller.simulateBatchSwap(
    [...oldA.map(i => i.id), ...oldB.map(i => i.id)],
    () => {
        teamA = oldB;
        teamB = oldA;
    }
);
```

---

## Constraints

- **Cannot run while a drag is in progress.** Both methods reject with an error if `controller.dragging` is `true`.
- **Item must exist in the DOM.** The item must be rendered in `fromContainerId` at the time the simulation starts.
- **No drop events by default.** Neither `onDrop` nor `onDragEnd` callbacks are called unless you pass `{ emitEvents: true }` to `simulateDrop`.

---

## Difference between methods

| | `simulateReturn` | `simulateDrop` | `simulateSwap` | `simulateBatchSwap` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Items animated | 1 | 1 | 2 | N |
| Simultaneous | — | — | ✓ | ✓ |
| Cross-container | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Fires drop events | No | Optional | No | No |
| Updates data | Caller, after | Caller, after | Caller, after | Caller, inside callback |
| Typical use case | Undo, cancel | Auto-sort (one-by-one) | Swap two items | Batch reorder, team swap |

